2025-05-01 03:18:52 - Thread-1318 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant tables, columns & why we need them
• pizza_clean_customer_orders.order_id – the order we received from a customer  
• pizza_clean_customer_orders.pizza_id – which pizza the customer bought  
• pizza_clean_customer_orders.exclusions – toppings the customer asked us to remove  
• pizza_clean_customer_orders.extras – toppings the customer asked us to add  
• pizza_clean_runner_orders.order_id – order that was actually picked up by a runner  
• pizza_clean_runner_orders.cancellation – if NULL the order was delivered, so we keep it  
• pizza_recipes.pizza_id – links a pizza to its default toppings list  
• pizza_recipes.toppings – comma‑separated list of default topping_id values  
• pizza_get_exclusions.order_id / exclusions – one row per topping the customer removed  
• pizza_get_extras.order_id / extras – one row per topping the customer added  
• pizza_toppings.topping_id – numeric code of a topping  
• pizza_toppings.topping_name – human readable ingredient name  

Typical conditions  
• Keep only delivered orders → pizza_clean_runner_orders.cancellation IS NULL  
• Join clean_customer_orders with clean_runner_orders on order_id  
• Join pizza_recipes on pizza_id to get the starting toppings for every pizza  
• Left join pizza_get_exclusions / pizza_get_extras to subtract or add toppings  
• Finally join pizza_toppings to translate topping_id to topping_name  
-------------------------------------------------------------------
Exploration queries (all different, each limited to 20 rows)

```sql
-- 1. Look at the list of pizzas we sell
SELECT DISTINCT pizza_id, pizza_name
FROM pizza_names
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect the master list of possible toppings
SELECT DISTINCT topping_id, topping_name
FROM pizza_toppings
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. See every pizza’s default recipe (comma‑separated topping codes)
SELECT DISTINCT pizza_id, toppings
FROM pizza_recipes
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Identify orders that were actually delivered (no cancellation)
SELECT DISTINCT r.order_id, r.runner_id, r.pickup_time
FROM pizza_clean_runner_orders AS r
WHERE r.cancellation IS NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Combine delivered orders with the pizza each customer bought
SELECT DISTINCT d.order_id,
       c.pizza_id,
       p.pizza_name
FROM pizza_clean_runner_orders AS d
JOIN pizza_clean_customer_orders AS c
  ON d.order_id = c.order_id
JOIN pizza_names AS p
  ON c.pizza_id = p.pizza_id
WHERE d.cancellation IS NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Display the default toppings list for every delivered order
SELECT DISTINCT c.order_id,
       p.toppings
FROM pizza_clean_runner_orders AS r
JOIN pizza_clean_customer_orders AS c
  ON r.order_id = c.order_id
JOIN pizza_recipes AS p
  ON c.pizza_id = p.pizza_id
WHERE r.cancellation IS NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Inspect what toppings customers explicitly removed
SELECT DISTINCT order_id,
       exclusions   -- single topping_id per row in this helper table
FROM pizza_get_exclusions
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Inspect what toppings customers explicitly added
SELECT DISTINCT order_id,
       extras      -- single topping_id per row in this helper table
FROM pizza_get_extras
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Put together an “order vs. topping_id” list *after* considering exclusions
--    (start with defaults and subtract exclusions)
SELECT DISTINCT o.order_id,
       TRIM(t.value) AS base_topping_id
FROM (
     SELECT c.order_id,
            p.toppings
     FROM pizza_clean_runner_orders  AS r
     JOIN pizza_clean_customer_orders AS c
       ON r.order_id = c.order_id
     JOIN pizza_recipes AS p
       ON c.pizza_id = p.pizza_id
     WHERE r.cancellation IS NULL
) AS o,
json_each('[' || o.toppings || ']') AS t   -- quick splitter for comma string
WHERE t.value NOT IN (
      SELECT CAST(exclusions AS TEXT) FROM pizza_get_exclusions
      WHERE order_id = o.order_id
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final aggregation: total servings of every topping used
SELECT t.topping_name,
       COUNT(*) AS total_quantity
FROM (
     -- derive the full list of topping_id actually used in every delivered order
     SELECT order_id, topping_id
     FROM (
         /* A. default toppings minus exclusions */
         SELECT o.order_id,
                TRIM(j.value) AS topping_id
         FROM (
              SELECT c.order_id, p.toppings
              FROM pizza_clean_runner_orders AS r
              JOIN pizza_clean_customer_orders AS c
                ON r.order_id = c.order_id
              JOIN pizza_recipes AS p
                ON c.pizza_id = p.pizza_id
              WHERE r.cancellation IS NULL
         ) AS o
         CROSS JOIN json_each('[' || o.toppings || ']') AS j
         WHERE j.value NOT IN (
               SELECT CAST(exclusions AS TEXT)
               FROM pizza_get_exclusions
               WHERE order_id = o.order_id
         )

         UNION ALL

         /* B. extras explicitly added */
         SELECT e.order_id,
                CAST(e.extras AS TEXT) AS topping_id
         FROM pizza_get_extras AS e
         JOIN pizza_clean_runner_orders AS r
           ON r.order_id = e.order_id
         WHERE r.cancellation IS NULL
     )
) AS used
JOIN pizza_toppings AS t
  ON CAST(used.topping_id AS INTEGER) = t.topping_id
GROUP BY t.topping_name
ORDER BY total_quantity DESC
LIMIT 20;
-- Description: Counts how many times each topping was finally used in delivered pizzas.
```

Running the last query will give the required summary in the requested “name / quantity” format.
